{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789387516,"nonce":"Xq5h2NQ18l1mz9kULbjBgV1Zb6o=","title":"Python: an instance check compares against every class deriving from the one wanted","message":"`Class$unbox` accepted a box by comparing its kind with each class in\nthe hierarchy below `Class`, one compare and one branch per class:\n`BaseException$unbox` was 35 blocks, and the sixteen exception\nunboxers together were the largest part of what every program lowered\nand compiled before it ran.\n\nClasses are now numbered in preorder of the hierarchy, each right after\nits base and before its base's next child, so a class and everything\nderiving from it hold one contiguous range of indices\n(`ClassInfo::descendants` is its length). `is_subclass` is a range\ntest and every unboxer is two compares whatever the hierarchy.\n\nFound while doing this: the coercion of a dynamic value to a class\n(`Class$unbox`) was not followed by a pending check, so a value of the\nwrong class left a TypeError pending and handed a null instance on,\nwhich the next read reported as AttributeError on None. It is guarded\nnow like the scalar unboxes; the `except X as e` binding, whose class\nthe match has settled, reads the instance without a check.\nConformance `class/hierarchy_order.py`.","files":null}]}